      "In the final lecture, the hall was over capacity and the windows were open, and he taught the only thing he had never put into words: the craft of continuation. How a field survives its professors. How to read a paper with the respect of a colleague and the scepticism of an heir. How to choose a question — not the answerable one, not the fashionable one, but the one that will still be interesting in thirty years, because your career is a marriage and the question is the spouse. 'You will be wrong most of your lives,' he said, in closing. 'That is the job description. The comfort is this: being wrong in good company, on important questions, for a long time — there is no better way to spend a mind. I have had thirty years of it. It was never once boring.'",
