      "Rahim Toodehroosta collected maps the way other men collect grievances: systematically, over decades, with an appetite that outlasted every practical purpose. His apartment above the framing shop held four thousand of them — county surveys, admiralty charts, a 1913 cadastral map of a village that no longer existed, and the pride of the collection, a hand-coloured plate of the Caspian coast with a mermaid where the cartographer had lost confidence in the coastline.",
      "His daughter, Nushin, a radiologist with a life built firmly in the present tense, had spent thirty years failing to understand the collection, and had settled, as families do, into affectionate détente. She brought him tea among the drawers. She listened to the lecture about the mermaid. She understood that the maps were, somehow, his way of holding the world still — her father, who had never travelled, who had turned down two postings abroad in the 1980s for reasons he declined to romanticise, owned every place he had never gone.",
      "The unfinished map was his last project and his secret. She found it after his death, in the flat drawer under the plan chest: a single sheet of heavy paper, on which her father had begun, in careful pencil, a map of his own neighbourhood — the streets as they had been in his childhood, drawn from memory. The date in the corner was eighteen years old. The map stopped, mid-street, at the corner where the bakery had stood. Four thousand maps of everywhere, and he had spent his last eighteen years drawing the four streets he had walked as a boy, and he had never finished.",
      "Nushin thought, initially, that it was failing memory — a final-years project begun too late. The pencil lines said otherwise. They were confident, even youthful; the lettering of a man with a draftsman's training (he had been, she kept forgetting, an engineer before the framing shop). The map didn't falter. It simply stopped, at the bakery corner, as if the cartographer had walked there in his mind, sat down on the curb, and decided to stay a while. Eighteen years on a curb.",
      "She took it to be appraised, because that is what one does, and the appraiser — a patient man of her father's generation — unrolled the sheet, adjusted his glasses, and was quiet for a long time. 'This isn't an unfinished map,' he said finally. 'You see the borders? He's drawn the frame of it complete. He ruled the neat line around the whole neighbourhood before he started filling it in. He knew exactly where it ended.' He looked at the empty two-thirds of the sheet, at the pencil streets with their confident names — names of shopkeepers, she realised, not street names; the baker's street was labelled with the baker's name. 'This is a map of what he kept,' the appraiser said. 'The rest of the paper is what he let go.'",
      "The collection was sold, eventually, in the orderly way of estates: the admiralty charts to a dealer in the capital, the Caspian plate to a museum that promised it light and climate control, the four thousand folded countrysides dispersed into the holdings of people who would love them, each, in their own incomplete way. The proceeds funded, at Nushin's direction, a radiology fellowship — the present tense, as she said at the dinner honouring it, being the only tense her father never mastered.",
      "The unfinished map stayed. It hangs now in her consulting room, framed exactly as it was, with the empty paper and the ruled border and the streets named for their shopkeepers, and it has become, over the years, a strange clinical instrument. Patients ask about it, the way patients ask about anything in a room where they must wait. She has learned to give the short version — my father's neighbourhood — and the long version, which she gives to the ones who ask twice, has been refined by repetition into something true: 'He collected the whole world, and in the end he drew one corner of it, and stopped where it was sweetest.'",
      "There was one more thing, discovered only when the framing shop was emptied: a second sheet, blank, ruled with the same neat border, folded into eighths and tucked into the drawer's corner — a prepared map, awaiting a second neighbourhood, or a second childhood, or nothing at all. Nushin kept it folded as he had kept it. It sits in her desk at home, under the radiology journals, and some evenings she takes it out and looks at the empty frame her father drew, complete and patient, around whatever he thought might come next.",
